  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 3.2. Increased capacity of member States to formulate and implement policies and strategies for creating decent work in the rural economy</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">1. In May 2021, sub regional-level tripartite constituents adopted and launched the Strategic Guidelines for the promotion of decent work in the cocoa productive chain (Cocoa 2030) in Brazil, in partnership with the World Cocoa Foundation (WCF). These guidelines were elaborated with the active participation of social partners contributing to reinforcing social dialogue. In response to the adoption of the Guidelines, WCF and its local counterpart Cocoa Action Brazil launched the Project Cocoa 2030, putting many of its directives into action through a US$ 1 million project financed by the private sector, and with the involvement of social partners. The guidelines emphasize the need of mobilizing the cocoa producers in relation to guaranteeing their rights and promoting strong trade unions &#x2013; a responsibility of the Confedera&#xE7;&#xE3;o Nacional dos Trabalhadores Rurais Agricultores e Agricultoras Familiares (CONTAG). CONTAG has joined the Project with the aim of strengthening its mandate and workers&apos; empowerment and representation within the Brazilian cocoa supply chain.

2. In August 2021, a Local Development Plan (LDP) for the promotion of Decent Work in the gypsum supply-chain was launched by the ILO and MPT in collaboration with the Global Compact and the Fundacao Getulio Vargas. The LDP was elaborated in a participatory way with the main actors of the supply chain and representatives from tripartite constituents. Consequently, the Government of the state of Pernambuco set up a Gypsum Sectoral Chamber with the participation of social partners and key stakeholders to promote decent work in the supply chain and with a view to implement the plan. 1. The ILO coordinated the collaboration with the United Nations Global Compact to conduct social dialogue roundtables with tripartite constituents (5 sessions), several bilateral meetings and multi-stakeholders consultations (focal groups, interviews, high-level meetings), including the organization of the Cocoa 2030 Workshop to build and validate the adopted guidelines. The adoption of the Guidelines results from the ILO&apos;s and the Labour Prosecution&#xB4;s Office (MPT) collaborative efforts, initiated in 2018, including the development of the study and documentary called &quot;A Situational analysis of the cocoa production chain: advances and challenges towards the promotion of decent work&quot;.
2. The LDP results from an intense ILO-led multistakeholder engagement effort, following an initial research by the ILO on the gypsum supply chain. The ILO actively supported the LDP process by promoting the establishment of the Gypsum 2030 Working Group, coordinating and leading the organization of the tripartite consultations and the coordination of national consultants that supported the LDP&apos;s elaboration.</narrative>
   </description>
